  10:Core/Src/startup.c ****     /*
  11:Core/Src/startup.c ****     naked 
  12:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - means no function epilogue(restores stack for parent) and no function prologue(this is where 
  13:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - you use this when you want to write function in assembly code(you can't use any local variabl
  14:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - you would typically write the prologue and epilogue in assembly
  15:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - The code in this function should be in assembly in a __asm__{code} statement
  16:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - Sometimes C code will work but its unreliable and should never be trusted(we get lucky here)
  17:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - Prologue also saves the current context like registers but as this function is 
  18:Core/Src/startup.c ****     run at the start the registers will have dummy data so storing them in RAM is a waste of time
  19:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - Epilogue also not necessary(an epilogue is generated even though no return) so naked
  20:Core/Src/startup.c ****     can save space
  21:Core/Src/startup.c ****     
  22:Core/Src/startup.c ****     
  23:Core/Src/startup.c ****     no_return
  24:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - this code will never go back to caller(except when there are exceptions in code)
  25:Core/Src/startup.c ****     difference to void is that function declared with void can go back to caller
  26:Core/Src/startup.c ****     - mainly used for optimisations like cutting out code that is written after the function call(t
  27:Core/Src/startup.c ****     */
